{"text":"Stronger multi-negative training is a credible SASRec improvement. [gSASRec’s IJCAI 2024 summary](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) combines multiple negatives with generalized BCE to reduce sampling-induced overconfidence; recommended settings are 128–256 negatives and calibration parameter t=0.75. [eSASRec (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.06450) identifies sampled softmax plus improved Transformer layers as a strong combination without extra item features, although its results do not isolate the loss’s contribution. These findings support testing multi-negative sampled softmax and gBCE before adding complex auxiliary contrastive objectives.

Hardness needs moderation. [R²NS (WWW 2026)](https://renzhaochun.github.io/assets/pdf/WWW_2026_Camera_Ready.pdf) reports early instability and false-negative risks from aggressive mining; it uses warm-start training, progressively adjusted difficulty, and stochastic selection among top-ranked candidates. Its experiments show deterministic hard-negative sampling can underperform uniform sampling. A practical adaptation: retain uniform negatives, introduce a modest mined fraction after warm-up, sample among several high-scoring candidates, and exclude observed training-history items and the current positive. This reduces known-positive collisions but cannot identify every latent positive.

For Electronics, these are hypotheses requiring validation. A [2024 sampling comparison](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17276) finds dataset-dependent trade-offs: popularity-based and in-batch negatives can balance popularity bands while lowering aggregate accuracy. Suggested ablation: existing BCE versus multi-negative sampled softmax versus gBCE, then add warmed-up stochastic mining to the best objective. Select exclusively by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10; inspect Electronics separately for regressions. Better training margins alone do not establish generalization, and none of these papers demonstrates gains under this exact 99-negative evaluator."}
